§ `Article 5(5): national rules`

-- "A Member State may decide to provide for the possibility to fully or partially authorise the use ... within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h),
-- and paragraphs 2 and 3." A grant limited by its own wording: a possibility outside those limits and conditions is not covered (NOTHING), not forbidden (split site (ii), fork F-5-5-may).
-- src:209 | 5. A Member State may decide to provide for the possibility to fully or partially authorise the use of ‘real-time’ remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for the purposes of law enforcement within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), and paragraphs 2 and 3. Member States concerned shall lay down in their national law the necessary detailed rules for the request, issuance and exercise of, as well as supervision and reporting relating to, the authorisations referred to in paragraph 3. Those rules shall also specify in respect of which of the objectives listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), including which of the criminal offences referred to in point (h)(iii) thereof, the competent authorities may be authorised to use those systems for the purposes of law enforcement. Member States shall notify those rules to the Commission at the latest 30 days following the adoption thereof. Member States may introduce, in accordance with Union law, more restrictive laws on the use of remote biometric identification systems.
@ref Art 5(5): a Member State may decide to provide for the possibility (src:209)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the use is of a 'real-time' remote biometric identification system in publicly accessible spaces for the purposes of law enforcement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the possibility is within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), and paragraphs 2 and 3` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Art 5(5) the Member State may provide for the possibility to authorise the use` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`)
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): Article 5 applies from 2 February 2025 (Article 113 third paragraph, point (a))
        IF NOT `the use is of a 'real-time' remote biometric identification system in publicly accessible spaces for the purposes of law enforcement`
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sentence is about that use
        IF `the possibility is within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), and paragraphs 2 and 3`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is "within the limits and under the conditions listed"; outside them the paragraph does not say the Member State may not

-- "Member States may introduce, in accordance with Union law, more restrictive laws on the use of remote biometric identification systems."
-- src:209 | 5. A Member State may decide to provide for the possibility to fully or partially authorise the use of ‘real-time’ remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for the purposes of law enforcement within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), and paragraphs 2 and 3. Member States concerned shall lay down in their national law the necessary detailed rules for the request, issuance and exercise of, as well as supervision and reporting relating to, the authorisations referred to in paragraph 3. Those rules shall also specify in respect of which of the objectives listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), including which of the criminal offences referred to in point (h)(iii) thereof, the competent authorities may be authorised to use those systems for the purposes of law enforcement. Member States shall notify those rules to the Commission at the latest 30 days following the adoption thereof. Member States may introduce, in accordance with Union law, more restrictive laws on the use of remote biometric identification systems.
@ref Art 5(5): more restrictive laws (src:209)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the more restrictive law is introduced in accordance with Union law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Art 5(5) the Member State may introduce more restrictive laws on the use of remote biometric identification systems` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`)
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): Article 5 applies from 2 February 2025 (Article 113 third paragraph, point (a))
        IF `the more restrictive law is introduced in accordance with Union law`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is "in accordance with Union law"; a law that is not so introduced is outside it, not forbidden by this sentence

-- src:209 | 5. A Member State may decide to provide for the possibility to fully or partially authorise the use of ‘real-time’ remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for the purposes of law enforcement within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), and paragraphs 2 and 3. Member States concerned shall lay down in their national law the necessary detailed rules for the request, issuance and exercise of, as well as supervision and reporting relating to, the authorisations referred to in paragraph 3. Those rules shall also specify in respect of which of the objectives listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), including which of the criminal offences referred to in point (h)(iii) thereof, the competent authorities may be authorised to use those systems for the purposes of law enforcement. Member States shall notify those rules to the Commission at the latest 30 days following the adoption thereof. Member States may introduce, in accordance with Union law, more restrictive laws on the use of remote biometric identification systems.
@ref Art 5(5): lay down the detailed rules (src:209)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the Member State has decided to provide for the possibility to authorise the use` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Role `Art 5(2) to (8) duty`
`Art 5(5) the duty of the Member State to lay down the detailed rules in national law` MEANS
    IF `Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked` AND `the Member State has decided to provide for the possibility to authorise the use`
    THEN
        PARTY Operator
        MUST `Art 5(5) lay down in national law the necessary detailed rules`
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY Operator BECAUSE "the necessary detailed rules were not laid down in national law"
        -- no time limit and no stated consequence (NOTES.md, rules with no breach path)
    ELSE FULFILLED  -- nothing is owed on these facts

-- "Member States shall notify those rules to the Commission at the latest 30 days following the adoption thereof." The clock starts at the adoption (day 0); the 30th day is in time.
-- src:209 | 5. A Member State may decide to provide for the possibility to fully or partially authorise the use of ‘real-time’ remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for the purposes of law enforcement within the limits and under the conditions listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), and paragraphs 2 and 3. Member States concerned shall lay down in their national law the necessary detailed rules for the request, issuance and exercise of, as well as supervision and reporting relating to, the authorisations referred to in paragraph 3. Those rules shall also specify in respect of which of the objectives listed in paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h), including which of the criminal offences referred to in point (h)(iii) thereof, the competent authorities may be authorised to use those systems for the purposes of law enforcement. Member States shall notify those rules to the Commission at the latest 30 days following the adoption thereof. Member States may introduce, in accordance with Union law, more restrictive laws on the use of remote biometric identification systems.
@ref Art 5(5): notify the rules within 30 days (src:209)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the Member State has adopted the national rules` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Role `Art 5(2) to (8) duty`
`Art 5(5) the duty of the Member State to notify the national rules to the Commission` MEANS
    IF `Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked` AND `the Member State has adopted the national rules`
    THEN
        PARTY Operator
        MUST `Art 5(5) notify the national rules to the Commission`
        WITHIN 30
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY Operator BECAUSE "the national rules were not notified to the Commission at the latest 30 days following their adoption"
    ELSE FULFILLED  -- nothing is owed on these facts

§ `Article 5(6) and (7): annual reports (the duties of officials)`

-- "shall submit to the Commission annual reports on such use": annual, with no date by which each report is due, and no consequence stated: no breach path.
-- The parties are national market surveillance authorities and data protection authorities; the Commission provides the template. Officials have no seat; the core has no constructors for them (NEEDS-CORE.md).
-- src:210 | 6. National market surveillance authorities and the national data protection authorities of Member States that have been notified of the use of ‘real-time’ remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes pursuant to paragraph 4 shall submit to the Commission annual reports on such use. For that purpose, the Commission shall provide Member States and national market surveillance and data protection authorities with a template, including information on the number of the decisions taken by competent judicial authorities or an independent administrative authority whose decision is binding upon requests for authorisations in accordance with paragraph 3 and their result.
@ref Art 5(6): annual reports to the Commission (src:210)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the authority has been notified of a use of a 'real-time' remote biometric identification system in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes pursuant to paragraph 4` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Role `Art 5(2) to (8) duty`
`Art 5(6) the duty of the authority to submit annual reports to the Commission` MEANS
    IF     `Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`
       AND `the authority has been notified of a use of a 'real-time' remote biometric identification system in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes pursuant to paragraph 4`
    THEN
        PARTY Operator
        MUST `Art 5(6) submit annual reports to the Commission`
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY Operator BECAUSE "the annual report on such use was not submitted to the Commission"
        -- annual, with no date by which each report is due, and no stated consequence (NOTES.md, rules with no breach path)
    ELSE FULFILLED  -- nothing is owed on these facts

-- src:210 | 6. National market surveillance authorities and the national data protection authorities of Member States that have been notified of the use of ‘real-time’ remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes pursuant to paragraph 4 shall submit to the Commission annual reports on such use. For that purpose, the Commission shall provide Member States and national market surveillance and data protection authorities with a template, including information on the number of the decisions taken by competent judicial authorities or an independent administrative authority whose decision is binding upon requests for authorisations in accordance with paragraph 3 and their result.
@ref Art 5(6): the Commission provides a template (src:210)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC Role `Art 5(2) to (8) duty`
`Art 5(6) the duty of the Commission to provide a template` MEANS
    IF `Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`
    THEN
        PARTY Operator
        MUST `Art 5(6) provide a template`
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY Operator BECAUSE "the template was not provided to Member States and to national market surveillance and data protection authorities"
        -- no time limit and no stated consequence (NOTES.md, rules with no breach path)
    ELSE FULFILLED  -- nothing is owed on these facts

-- src:211 | 7. The Commission shall publish annual reports on the use of real-time remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes, based on aggregated data in Member States on the basis of the annual reports referred to in paragraph 6. Those annual reports shall not include sensitive operational data of the related law enforcement activities.
@ref Art 5(7): the Commission publishes annual reports (src:211)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC Role `Art 5(2) to (8) duty`
`Art 5(7) the duty of the Commission to publish annual reports` MEANS
    IF `Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`
    THEN
        PARTY Operator
        MUST `Art 5(7) publish annual reports`
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH BY Operator BECAUSE "the annual report on the use of real-time remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes was not published"
        -- annual, with no date by which each report is due, and no stated consequence (NOTES.md, rules with no breach path); the sensitive-operational-data sentence is a limit on the content of the report (see below)
    ELSE FULFILLED  -- nothing is owed on these facts

-- "Those annual reports shall not include sensitive operational data of the related law enforcement activities": a flat negative, a bar on the content of the reports.
-- src:211 | 7. The Commission shall publish annual reports on the use of real-time remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement purposes, based on aggregated data in Member States on the basis of the annual reports referred to in paragraph 6. Those annual reports shall not include sensitive operational data of the related law enforcement activities.
@ref Art 5(7): no sensitive operational data in the reports (src:211)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the report includes sensitive operational data of the related law enforcement activities` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Art 5(7) the report is barred from including sensitive operational data` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`)
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): Article 5 applies from 2 February 2025 (Article 113 third paragraph, point (a))
        IF `the report includes sensitive operational data of the related law enforcement activities`
        THEN JUST TRUE  -- "Those annual reports shall not include sensitive operational data of the related law enforcement activities"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a report with no such data is not what the bar is about

§ `Article 5(8): other Union law`

-- "This Article shall not affect the prohibitions that apply where an AI practice infringes other Union law."
-- JUST FALSE: Article 5 does not affect a prohibition that applies under other Union law (a flat negative); NOTHING where the practice does not infringe other Union law (the sentence is silent).
-- src:212 | 8. This Article shall not affect the prohibitions that apply where an AI practice infringes other Union law.
@ref Art 5(8): other Union law (src:212)
GIVEN `the date asked` IS A DATE
      `the AI practice infringes other Union law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Art 5(8) Article 5 affects the prohibitions that apply under other Union law` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`Art 5 the Chapter II provisions apply on the date asked` `the date asked`)
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): Article 5 applies from 2 February 2025 (Article 113 third paragraph, point (a))
        IF `the AI practice infringes other Union law`
        THEN JUST FALSE  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This Article shall not affect the prohibitions that apply where an AI practice infringes other Union law"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the sentence is about a practice that infringes other Union law
